The command @code{agent-menu} opens the unified transient menu. Every entry in it works with either backend: session switching, waiting-session jumps, resuming a past session, creating a branch of the current session, switching between branches, handoff, restart, exit, history browsing, skill execution, Trajectory CR task worktree creation, project audit, one dispatcher that acts on the thing at point, and a separate Prompts section for prompt capture, captured prompt insertion, and batch TODO processing.

@findex agent-history
The command @code{agent-history}, bound to @samp{l} in the Sessions column, opens the Agent Log menu for browsing, opening, searching, and resuming historical sessions across backends. Agent Log is an optional package: loading @samp{agent} never requires it, @code{agent-history} loads it on demand, and invoking the command without Agent Log installed signals a clear error. Neither @code{agent-menu} nor the session switcher reads any historical catalog while opening; catalog work (which for Codex can start an app-server process and take seconds) happens only after an explicit Agent Log action.

In EAT-backed Claude and Codex session buffers, @code{agent-setup-scroll-keys} makes PageUp and PageDown send terminal mouse-wheel events, matching the TUI scroll path used by the mouse. PageUp and PageDown in other selected buffers retain their normal bindings and never target a visible AI terminal in another window.

The command @code{agent-start-or-switch} is the main entry point. If no sessions exist, it starts a new session through the selected backend. If sessions already exist, it opens the session switcher. Sessions receive stable home-row keys where possible and display backend icons next to their labels.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-functions
Sessions can also carry a one-line annotation after their name, shown dimmed in the @code{agent-session-annotation} face. The text comes from @code{agent-session-annotation-functions}, an abnormal hook: each function is called with a session buffer and returns a string, or @code{nil} to leave the session to the functions after it. The first non-nil answer is the one shown, and the functions after it are not called. @samp{agent} renders whatever it returns and knows nothing about where the text comes from. Whitespace in the answer is collapsed to single spaces, so a multi-line answer cannot break the menu's layout, and an answer that is blank or not a string counts as no annotation. Sessions without an annotation — including sessions every function declines to annotate — render exactly as they did before annotations existed. The default is @code{nil}, which shows session names alone; Agent Log adds a function that supplies each session's stored one-line summary (@uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log, agent-log}), and with no such integration loaded the switcher looks unchanged. Because this is a hook, an integration adds itself with @code{add-hook} rather than taking a slot over: a function you installed yourself keeps working whichever loads first, and @code{remove-hook} takes either one back out.

Annotations line up in one column across every account group: the labels of annotated sessions are padded to the widest of those labels, measured in display columns, so a name containing double-width characters does not push its annotation to the right. Sessions without an annotation are left unpadded.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-max-width
Annotations are truncated with an ellipsis to fit the switcher window, which is as wide as the frame. The fit subtracts everything the line spends around the annotation: the columns to the left of the Sessions column, the key and the padding transient formats a suffix with, the padded label, one separator column, and a two-column right margin. A frame too narrow for that budget yields a short annotation rather than none, since the fit never drops below twenty columns. Setting @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} to an integer caps the result (@ref{Shared session behavior}); it can only narrow an annotation, never widen it past what the frame holds. A cap that leaves no room — that is, @samp{0} — renders the bare label instead.

A function added to @code{agent-session-annotation-functions} must be cheap and free of side effects: building the switcher calls it more than once per session, because aligning the annotations needs every label's width before any label is final. Reading a value from memory is appropriate; reading a file or contacting a process is not.

The command @code{agent-run-skill} discovers skills from every loaded backend that implements skill discovery. It disambiguates same-named skills by backend label, prompts for declared arguments when a skill frontmatter requests them, and dispatches the invocation to the selected backend. Claude and Codex backends scan global and project-local programmatic skill directories, using both @code{project-current} and nearby tool directories as project-root signals, so batch-only skills can be run from this command without being loaded into ordinary interactive agent sessions.

The command @code{agent-capture-prompt} opens a session-specific Org file and appends a new prompt entry. The command @code{agent-insert-captured-prompt} reads pending nonempty entries for the current session, prompts for one with a short body preview in the completion candidate, inserts its text into the backend prompt without submitting it, and removes the selected Org entry from the capture file. With a prefix argument, it includes legacy entries already marked as inserted. When a session still has pending captured prompts, @code{agent-exit}, @code{agent-restart}, and @code{agent-handoff} ask for confirmation before closing or replacing that session.

The command @code{agent-post-push-ci} runs the @code{post-push-ci} skill through the
selected backend with @samp{--no-push --commit HEAD}. Use it after a push has
already happened and you want an agent to inspect GitHub Actions failures, fix
the root cause, and continue the authorized CI closeout loop.

The command @code{agent-trajectory-new-task} creates a Trajectory @samp{reasoning-tasks}
task worktree from @samp{origin/main} at @samp{~/Trajectory/reasoning-tasks/<slug>},
checks out branch @samp{pablo/<slug>}, wires the worktree's @samp{.claude/.env} symlink to the canonical
Rubric Studio key, sparse-checks out the worktree to @samp{.claude}, @samp{meta} and
@samp{platform} (keeping the checkout small instead of materializing the whole task
corpus), runs the sync hook script configured by
@code{agent-trajectory-sync-worktree-script}, and opens the new worktree in Dired.
The sync script is the same SessionStart hook every later session runs, and
the single source of truth for composing the local @samp{AGENTS.md} and @samp{CLAUDE.md}
instruction overlay, linking the canonical key and injecting private skills.
The command is the Emacs equivalent of the shell @samp{newtask} helper, but it does
not depend on the local @samp{main} worktree's current branch.

The command @code{agent-act-on-forge-notification} is the Forge counterpart of
@code{agent-act-on-slack-message}. Called on a notification in
@code{forge-list-notifications}, it starts a session in the working tree of the
repository that notification belongs to and inserts the issue or pull request
URL into the prompt without submitting it. It also accepts the topic at point,
so it works from a topic buffer or a Magit status buffer.

Unlike Slack routing, it needs no language model and no project registry. A
Slack message has to be classified to find its project, whereas a Forge
notification already records its repository and the repository already records
its working tree. The command signals an error when the repository has no local
clone, or when the recorded path no longer exists: Forge does not update that
path when a repository is renamed or moved, so a stale entry is an ordinary
failure rather than an exceptional one.

@findex agent-act-on-thing-at-point
The menu binds no routing command directly. @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point}, bound to @samp{.} in the Tools column, walks @code{agent-at-point-actions} and calls the first command whose predicate matches the current buffer: @code{agent-debug-backtrace} in a @samp{*Backtrace*} buffer, @code{agent-act-on-slack-message} in a Slack room buffer, @code{agent-act-on-forge-notification} on a Forge notification or topic, and @code{agent-send-todo-at-point} on an org TODO heading. Every predicate answers from the buffer alone — the buffer name, the buffer-local variable @samp{slack} sets on room buffers, the major mode, the org TODO state — so dispatch never loads a module to ask whether it applies, and the Forge lookup runs only in a Magit-derived buffer. When nothing matches, the command signals an error naming the four kinds it recognizes. The four commands stay interactive, so any of them can still be bound directly in the mode where it applies.

The commands @code{agent-handoff}, @code{agent-audit-project}, @code{agent-debug-backtrace}, @code{agent-act-on-slack-message}, @code{agent-exit}, and @code{agent-restart} resolve a backend from the current session buffer or by prompting. This lets the same keybinding operate on Claude or Codex sessions without separate wrapper commands. The old per-backend command names (@code{agent-claude-handoff}, @code{agent-codex-restart}, and so on) remain available as obsolete aliases.

@code{agent-handoff} reads the backend's entry in @code{agent-handoff-files}, kills the current session buffer, and starts a new session with the handoff prompt. @code{agent-handoff-from-emacsclient} accepts the source buffer name from an @samp{emacsclient} argument, so agent-authored handoff commands can close the requesting session even though Emacs evaluates the form outside that buffer. If the source buffer name is unavailable, handoff closes the single existing session buffer for the handoff directory before starting the replacement session, which avoids the CLI's instance-name prompt in unattended loops.

@code{agent-restart} kills the current session buffer and resumes the same session in place, preserving the session's directory, instance name, account, and any launch state the backend's @code{:restart-options} function captures. It prompts when the active account differs from the session account, and it leaves the account unset when neither the session nor the global selection names one, so a session that ran under the backend's ambient default home is restarted there rather than pushed onto an account it never used.  Every precondition is checked before the old session is killed: restart refuses when a named account is no longer configured, when the account is logged out, or when no resumable session identity exists, and in each case the running session survives untouched. @code{agent-audit-project} prompts for a project directory and runs each skill in @code{agent-audit-skills} sequentially through the backend's non-interactive prompt runner. @code{agent-debug-backtrace} saves the current Emacs backtrace, asks @samp{gptel} to identify likely packages, and starts a session in the selected package source directory with instructions to fix the bug. @code{agent-setup-kill-on-exit} arranges for a session buffer to be killed when its process exits, consulting the backend's @code{:before-kill-check} slot first. It is not a menu entry: each backend installs it, along with @code{agent-disable-scrollback-truncation} and, for Codex, @code{agent-fix-rendering}, from its session-start hook, so the menu carries no buffer-maintenance commands.

@code{agent-before-exit-functions} can delay @code{agent-exit} for cleanup work.
Each function receives the resolved backend and session buffer; returning nil
aborts the exit.
Once a forced session close proceeds, Agent kills the target buffer without
running buffer-local kill-query prompts, so stale session buffers do not survive
restart or exit workflows.

Set @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name} (a single skill) or
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} (an ordered list) to submit interactive skills
before exiting matching sessions. Each entry in @code{agent-before-exit-skill-names}
is either a skill-name string or a list whose car is the skill name and whose
cdr is a plist accepting @code{:directories} (restrict where that skill runs,
overriding @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories}) and @code{:args} (a string
appended to the submitted command). For example:

@lisp
(setq agent-before-exit-skill-names
      '("session-learning-capture"
        ("update-log" :directories ("~/My Drive/Epoch") :args "--auto")))
@end lisp

@code{agent-run-skill-before-exit} uses each backend's registered skill command
prefix so Claude sessions receive slash commands and Codex sessions receive
dollar-prefixed skill commands, then submits the first applicable skill. The skills run in
order, each submitted after the previous one finishes, and the session exits
after the last; ordinary ready notifications are left unchanged while the chain
runs. When @code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} is nil, @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name}
is used as a single-skill fallback. Leave @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories}
nil to run an unscoped skill before exiting every session.
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-min-duration-seconds} defaults to 60 seconds, so very
short sessions skip the before-exit skills when the backend can report elapsed
session time.
Set the buffer-local variable @code{agent-before-exit-skill-inhibit} non-nil for
orchestration sessions that must close immediately without running the
configured before-exit skill. Handoff commands set this automatically on the
source session before closing it.

The chain is driven by session stop events and guarded by a watchdog. Each
submitted skill gets up to @code{agent-before-exit-timeout} seconds (default 600) to
finish; the watchdog restarts when the next skill begins. If the current skill
exceeds that interval, the chain is abandoned with a warning and the session is
left open instead of being stranded.

Backends can veto that automatic close while the submitted skill invocation is
still visible as unaccepted prompt input. Codex uses this to avoid closing a
session when the dollar-prefixed skill name was inserted but not submitted,
while ignoring submitted command scrollback, visible placeholder text, and
autosuggestion text. The readiness check recognises the standard Codex prompt
markers (@samp{›} and @samp{❯}); a chain that stalls after the first skill usually means
the running Codex renders an unrecognised prompt marker.

Backends can provide an atomic submit operation for before-exit commands.
Claude and Codex both target the resolved session buffer directly, insert the
skill command, and submit it once before the terminal buffer is displayed
again. Codex delegates this to @samp{codex.el}'s target-buffer submit helper.

@node Branch navigation
@section Branch navigation

@findex agent-resume
@findex agent-create-branch
@findex agent-switch-branch
A branch is a session forked from another one: it starts with the parent's whole conversation and then diverges. Branching lets you try a different approach without losing the state you built up, and the three commands here work the same way whichever backend the session belongs to.

@code{agent-create-branch}, bound to @samp{N}, forks the session in the current buffer and opens the fork in its own buffer under an instance name derived from the time of the fork. By default the branch shares the parent's working tree, which is what starting a second session in the same project would give you. With a prefix argument it also creates a git worktree on a fresh branch under @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} and runs the fork there, so that destructive git operations in one branch cannot disturb the other. The worktree starts at the parent's HEAD, so uncommitted parent changes are not carried into it; use the isolated form when concurrent git work across branches is a real concern, and the default otherwise. Before the fork starts, the backend's @code{:prepare-fork} slot makes the parent session visible from the new directory, which is what a backend that stores its transcripts per project directory needs in order to resume a session recorded elsewhere.

@code{agent-switch-branch}, bound to @samp{B}, shows every session sharing a fork ancestor with the current one as an indented tree, each entry carrying its first prompt and its timestamp and the session you are in marked with an asterisk, and switches to the selected session: to its live buffer when it still has one, and otherwise by resuming it in a new buffer. It reports that there is nothing to navigate when the current session has no relatives.

@code{agent-resume}, bound to @samp{R}, resumes a past session of the current backend, or of a backend it asks for when no session buffer names one, and passes its prefix argument through to the backend, where it means the most recent session.

Both backends supply the lineage these commands read, through the @code{:session-headers} slot, and each derives it from what its CLI already records. Claude Code writes one transcript per session inside a per-project directory, so the scan reads the first line of each transcript in the project the current session belongs to. Codex writes one rollout file per session, all under a single sessions directory, so the scan reads the @code{session_meta} record opening each rollout, keeps the ones whose recorded working directory matches, and takes the parent from @code{forked_from_id}. Because that directory holds every Codex session on the machine, the scan is bounded by a start timestamp when the caller only needs one session's descendants.

Codex records subagent threads with the same @code{forked_from_id} field, and those are deliberately left out of the tree. They are forks as far as the file format is concerned, but they are not branches of your conversation: they are threads the model spawned to do work on its own, and offering to switch into one would be offering something a user never chose to create.

@node Org TODO commands
@section Org TODO commands

@findex agent-batch-todos
@findex agent-send-todo-at-point
The @samp{agent-todo} module turns Org TODO entries into work for an AI session, and both of its commands work with either backend.

@code{agent-send-todo-at-point}, bound to @samp{t}, formats the heading and body of the TODO at point as a prompt and submits it to the session for the current file's project, asking which one when no single session can be inferred. When @code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword} is set, the heading moves to that keyword once the prompt is on its way.

@code{agent-batch-todos}, bound to @samp{b}, collects several TODO entries and runs them one after another without an interactive session. It infers its scope by itself — the region when one is active, the subtree when the buffer is narrowed, and the whole buffer otherwise — then asks for a working directory, and confirms the number of entries before starting unless you selected them as a region, where the selection is confirmation enough. Each entry runs through the resolved backend's @code{:exec-prompt} slot, its raw output is written to a numbered file under a timestamped directory in @code{agent-todo-log-directory}, and a summary buffer collects every entry's status, duration, cost, and output when the queue is empty.

Cost appears per entry only for backends whose CLI reports it. Claude's non-interactive runs report a dollar figure and it is shown; @samp{codex exec} reports none, so Codex entries render an em dash in place of a cost and contribute nothing to the total.

@node Chief-of-staff commands
@section Chief-of-staff commands

@findex agent-chief-start
@findex agent-chief-start-session
@findex agent-chief-stop
@findex agent-chief-tick
@findex agent-chief-stateless-tick
@findex agent-chief-session-heartbeat
@findex agent-chief-switch-to-session
@findex agent-chief-open-state
@findex agent-chief-add-note
@findex agent-chief-set-day-plan
The command @code{agent-chief-start-session} starts or reuses a dedicated Claude Code or Codex session using @code{agent-chief-session-instance-name}, sends the chief-of-staff operating contract into the session, asks the model to request today's plan there, and starts the heartbeat timer. The command @code{agent-chief-switch-to-session} jumps back to that session so the user can reply normally.

The command @code{agent-chief-start} starts only the repeating Emacs timer. Without a prefix argument it also schedules an immediate tick; with a prefix argument, it waits @code{agent-chief-interval} seconds before the first tick. @code{agent-chief-stop} cancels the timer. @code{agent-chief-tick} runs one check using @code{agent-chief-mode}. @code{agent-chief-session-heartbeat} forces a session-mode heartbeat, while @code{agent-chief-stateless-tick} forces one stateless JSON-decision check.

The command @code{agent-chief-open-state} opens the auditable Org state file. @code{agent-chief-add-note} appends a manual note, and @code{agent-chief-set-day-plan} appends today's plan. When a chief session is active, both commands also submit the new context into that session. These commands are intentionally simple: the durable source of truth remains ordinary text that can be reviewed, edited, and audited outside the model.

@node Claude Code commands
@section Claude Code commands

@findex agent-claude-start-or-switch
@findex agent-claude-select-account
@findex agent-claude-init-account
@findex agent-claude-start-status-polling
@findex agent-claude-stop-status-polling
@findex agent-claude-ensure-statusline-config
@findex agent-claude-ensure-stop-hook-config
@findex agent-claude-ensure-notification-hook-config
@findex agent-claude-setup-config
The Claude commands expose workflows that are specific to Claude Code; handoff, restart, exit, resume, branch navigation, skill execution, project audits, backtrace debugging, TODO workflows, and Slack-message routing use the unified @code{agent-*} commands described above. @code{agent-claude-start-or-switch} starts Claude under the selected account or opens the unified switcher. @code{agent-claude-select-account} persists an account choice, and @code{agent-claude-init-account} prepares a new account directory with shared configuration files and symlinks.

The branch and TODO commands that used to be Claude's own are now the shared @code{agent-create-branch}, @code{agent-switch-branch}, @code{agent-batch-todos}, and @code{agent-send-todo-at-point}; @code{agent-claude-create-branch}, @code{agent-claude-switch-branch}, @code{agent-claude-batch-todos}, and @code{agent-claude-send-todo-at-point} remain as obsolete aliases.

@code{agent-claude-start-status-polling} and @code{agent-claude-stop-status-polling} start and stop status polling for the current Claude buffer by hand. Polling is started and stopped for you with the session, so these are maintenance commands rather than part of a normal workflow; they are available through @samp{M-x} and are not in @code{agent-menu}.

The setup command @code{agent-claude-setup-config} ensures that Claude's statusline, Stop hook, and Notification hook are present in @samp{~/.claude/settings.json}. The backend does not run setup automatically when loaded; run the setup command explicitly when installing or repairing hook configuration.

@node Codex commands
@section Codex commands

@findex agent-codex-start-or-switch
@findex agent-codex-select-account
@findex agent-codex-resume
@findex agent-codex-fork
The Codex commands expose workflows that are specific to the Codex CLI; handoff, restart, exit, skill execution, project audits, backtrace debugging, and Slack-message routing use the unified @code{agent-*} commands described above. @code{agent-codex-start-or-switch} starts Codex under the selected account when no Codex sessions exist, otherwise it opens the shared switcher. @code{agent-codex-select-account} persists an account choice by name and applies it to new interactive sessions and non-interactive @samp{codex exec} runs. Skills run non-interactively with @samp{codex exec}, using local skill metadata when available and falling back to a slash invocation otherwise; additional programmatic skill directories are configured through @code{agent-codex-programmatic-skill-directories}.

When @code{agent-restart} operates on a Codex session, it resumes codex.el's canonical session identity under the chosen account and current default @code{codex-terminal-backend}; app-server sessions use the native app-server resume path and terminal backends use @samp{codex resume}. Changing @code{codex-terminal-backend} and then restarting a Codex session switches the relaunched session to that backend. The unified commands cover both Codex resuming and Codex forking: @code{agent-resume} resumes a past session of whichever backend the context names, and @code{agent-create-branch} forks the current session of either backend, reaching Codex through @samp{codex fork} on the terminal backends and @samp{thread/fork} on the app-server backend. @code{agent-codex-resume} and @code{agent-codex-fork} still expose the CLI's own subcommands and remain available through @samp{M-x}, though neither is in @code{agent-menu} any more. @code{agent-codex-fork} is not redundant: it forks a session you pick from Codex's own history, whereas @code{agent-create-branch} forks the session you are sitting in.

